WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.060
Mind your decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:06.810
I'm thinking of two regular polygons

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:15.088
one polygon has m sides and D(m) diagonals and, the other has n sides and D(n) diagonals

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.520
If D(m) plus D(n) is equal to one hundred and twenty five,

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.460
solve for the value of m plus n

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.260
A version of this problem was asked in the 2018 MATHCOUNTS National Competition

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:34.120
Students have less than one minute to solve this problem without the use of a calculator.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:41.669
I admit it took me about five minutes to solve. So, I think it's okay if you take longer to solve this problem.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.939
I thank Patrick from Germany for the suggestion

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:52.080
Pause if you'd like to give this problem a try and when you're ready, keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.860
I'm first going to present two formulas for the number of diagonals in a polygon with k sides

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.260
where k is greater than or equal to three

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.880
I'll then illustrate how to solve the problem quickly

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:17.160
One formula is an explicit formula

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:21.490
D(k) is equal to k multiplied by k minus 3 all over 2

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:27.309
This is useful if you want to evaluate a particular value like k is equal to one hundred

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.960
Another formula is known as the recursive formula.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.780
This is D(k) is equal to D(k) minus one plus k minus two

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.420
This is useful for computing the next value if you know the previous value

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.780
This is useful if you want to create a table, which is what we need to do in this problem.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.810
Before I get to that let me prove both of these formulas

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:54.080
I'll first prove the explicit formula by direct counting.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.900
In a polygon with k sides, we have k vertices

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.860
How many diagonals can we draw from a particular vertex?

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:06.999
We'll notice that the adjacent vertices will form sides with this vertex

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.839
So these two sides will not be diagonals of this polygon.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.820
We also cannot draw diagonals between the vertex and itself,

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:20.280
so these three vertices will be excluded in the count of drawing diagonals from this vertex.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:25.239
But every other vertex will form a diagonal with this vertex.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:30.580
Therefore, we have k minus 3 diagonals that we can draw from this vertex.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:35.500
If we do this for all k vertices, we have k multiplied by k minus three

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.400
Now, we have to make one more adjustment.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:42.060
Every single diagonal involves two different vertices.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.780
To avoid double counting, we therefore have to divide this by two

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:53.140
And thus, we have k multiplied by k minus three all over two and that's one way to prove the explicit formula

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.920
Another way to prove it is by considering the complete graph

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.960
Consider k vertices as before

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.060
Now, let's connect every pair of vertices with an edge

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.900
In total we will have k choose two edges

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:11.820
because every pair of vertices will make an edge

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:17.070
Now, every edge is either a side or a diagonal of this polygon

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.210
but remember, there are k sides to this polygon.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:26.760
If we subtract the second equation from the first we get a count of the number of diagonals

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.140
k choose two is equal to k multiplied by k minus one, over two

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.040
and when we subtract k,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.300
we get k multiplied by k minus three all over two

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.420
and thus we've proven the formula in another way

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.660
So now, let me prove the recursive formula

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:48.180
Suppose we have k minus one vertices and we have a formula that counts the number of diagonals

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:52.600
How many more diagonals will we have, if we add one more vertex?

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.760
That is, if we have a k-sided polygon with k vertices.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:02.100
Well, as before these three cases will not be diagonals of this new polygon

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:06.220
But every other connection will be a diagonal of the polygon.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:10.140
So, we need to add on k minus 3 more diagonals.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.960
Now, if we connect the adjacent vertices to make sides of this polygon,

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:22.320
let's examine what happens to this previous side of the previous polygon with k minus one vertices

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.080
We'll notice that this will actually be a diagonal of the new polygon.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.860
So, we need to add one more to this formula

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:36.960
The result is that D(k) is equal to D(k) minus one plus, k minus three plus, one.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.740
And that simplifies to be D(k) minus one plus, k minus two.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:45.680
And that's the recursive formula we'll need to solve this problem quickly

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:48.620
So now, let's solve the problem quickly.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.700
For fun, let's see how long it takes.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.100
We'll first create a table for the number of diagonals

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.160
A triangle with three sides has zero diagonals; a quadrilateral has two diagonals

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:02.000
To get D(5), we take D(4) plus three.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:07.380
This will be equal to five; then, D(6) is equal to D(5) plus four so, we get nine.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:14.060
To get, seven we add five; to get eight, we add 6 and we quickly compute all of the values in this table

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:17.800
We want to exceed one hundred and twenty five diagonals.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:22.320
Then, we look for a pair that has a sum of 125

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:26.540
This will be thirty five plus ninety corresponding to D(10) plus D(15)

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.660
Therefore, m plus n is equal to ten plus fifteen which is equal to twenty five.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:34.980
And that's our answer! And we solved it in just under 40 seconds!

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.960
I think this is a really fun problem.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:43.150
Thanks for making "Mind Your Decisions" one of the best channels on YouTube.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:47.079
As always thanks for watching and thanks for your support

